Implementing Consensus Algorithms, 2PC and Raft 

Q1 and Q2 

How to Run the System

Prerequisites

- Docker and Docker Compose must be installed on your machine.

Steps

1. Build the Docker Images

   - For the Python Voting Service:
     
     cd q1_python
     docker build -t twopc-voting:latest .
     

   - For the Node.js Decision Service:
  
     cd q2_node
     docker build -t twopc-decision:latest .
     

2. Prepare the Docker Compose File

   Use the provided `docker-compose.yml` file (located in root directory) which defines containers for all 5 nodes and the coordinator services.

3. Run the System

   docker-compose up

4. Prepare the Docker Compose File

	•	Phase Voting of Node <nodeX> receives RPC Vote from Phase Voting of Node coordinator
	•	Phase Voting of Node <nodeX> sends RPC ReceiveVote to Phase Decision of Node nodeX_decision:50052
	•	Phase Decision of Node coordinatorNode sends RPC GetVote to Phase Decision of Node nodeX_decision:50052
	•	Phase Decision of Node coordinatorNode sends RPC GlobalDecision to Phase Decision of Node nodeX_decision:50052
	•	Transaction tx354: Final decision applied: COMMIT/ABORT


Q3: 
Prerequisites:
Ensure you have the following installed on your machine:
• Python 3.9+
• Docker 

How to Compile and Run
Python (Q3) – Leader Election
Navigate to Q3_python:
cd Q3_python

Build the Docker images:

docker compose build

Start the 5-node Python cluster:
docker compose up
Observe the logs. Within a few seconds, one Python node (1..5) should declare itself leader.

Q4: 
Prerequisites:
Ensure you have the following installed on your machine:
• Go 1.18+
• Docker, grpcurl


 Go (Q4) – Log Replication

Open a new terminal, go to Q4_golang:
cd ../Q4_golang

Build the Docker images:

docker compose build

Start the 5-node Go cluster:

docker compose up

You will see logs for nodes 6..10. Since we rely on the Python leader, these Go nodes typically remain followers and wait for heartbeats from whichever Python node is leader.

Confirm Cross-Communication
the Python leader will send heartbeats and log entries to the Go nodes.

Check the logs on a Go node (e.g. node6) to see something like:

[Node 6] runs RPC AppendEntries called by Node 3 (Term 1)
[Node 6] Heartbeat/log accepted from leader 3 (Term 1). Log size = 0, commitIndex=0


Sending a Client Request
To test log replication from the Python leader to the Go nodes:

Identify the Python leader. Suppose it’s node5 and you mapped port 50055:50055 in the Python docker-compose.yml.

From your host (assuming you have grpcurl installed), run:

docker exec -it python_node1 /bin/sh
apk add --no-cache grpcurl


grpcurl -plaintext \
  -proto raft.proto \
  -d '{"operation":"PUT x=5"}' \
  node5:50055 \
  raft.Raft/SendClientRequest

Observe the Python leader logs:

[Node 5] (LEADER) appended operation 'PUT x=5' ...
sends RPC AppendEntries to Node 6 ...

Observe the Go logs, e.g. node6:

[Node 6] Heartbeat/log accepted from leader 5 (Term X). Log size = 1, commitIndex=1
This shows the log entry was replicated to the Go side.




References:
Raft Paper: “In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm” by Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout.
Official Go gRPC Docs: https://grpc.io/docs/languages/go
Official Python gRPC Docs: https://grpc.io/docs/languages/python
Protobuf: https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers
In order to refine the design and implementation details of our system, we consulted ChatGPT (OpenAI, 2023) for suggestions and clarifications on various technical aspects.
